 "fine": [
  "Measured on a desktop PC, not on a dedicated server. Your own numbers will be different; what matters on this page is the gap between the servers on the same machine.",
  "In the tests with 300 players the bot clients run inside the server's Java process and connect to it over the network like a real game client; their own CPU and memory use is left out of the numbers.",
  "In those tests the bots' own logic (moving like a client against the blocks around them) runs on the server's threads, which a real player's game never does. Since 18 November the bots keep the shapes of the blocks they walk against instead of looking each one up every tick, so that logic costs every server less than in earlier runs. They also ask for a view distance of 8 chunks, like a typical player (2 before 18 November, which made players near a chunk border appear and disappear for each other all the time). Since 20 November they also reuse the blocks around them while they stay in the same spot and no longer wake their own network thread from the server's thread on every tick (a separate thread of the bot plugin does that), so the bots charge every server even less. All servers in those tests ran with the same bots. What the bots allocate is measured on every bot tick and left out of the server's memory numbers. The redstone, fighting and cow pen tests use a separate bot program.",
  "Vex uses Alternate Current redstone by default (a faster redstone engine that Paper ships as an option); CanvasMC and Folia use vanilla redstone by default. The extra \u201c+ Alternate Current\u201d runs show both of them with that same setting turned on.",
  "During most of the Vex and CanvasMC runs on 14 November a game was running on the same PC; the Folia runs on 15 November had the PC to themselves. That background load can only have made Vex's and CanvasMC's numbers higher, not lower.",
  "During the runs of 20 November (Vex and CanvasMC in the 300-player tests, and Vex in the redstone, fighting and cow pen tests) a game was open in the background on the same PC, using about one CPU core. It ran during every server's runs, which took turns, and can only have made the numbers higher, not lower.",
  "Every server ran with its own default settings, including the optimizations it turns on by default. Tuning can improve any of them; these are the numbers you get out of the box.",
  "Folia was measured on Minecraft 26.1.2, the version of its newest stable build; Vex and CanvasMC on 26.2.",
  "Every server ran with only the bench plugins installed. A real server with many plugins spends extra time in those plugins on any software.",
  "Folia could not run the tests with 300 or more players: in three tries it never got more than 140 to 180 of the 300 bots online. Folia waits for a network thread on every login, and all 8 of its network threads were busy sending to the players already online (about 5 CPU cores with 140 players), so the other bots timed out while logging in, also when they joined 10 per second instead of 100 every 8 seconds. Vex and CanvasMC got all 300 bots in with the same bots and settings.",
  "In CanvasMC's 300 players moving run 1, 13 of the 300 bots timed out and left (the server ran at about 3 TPS); that run is still counted.",
